#solution(6, [Mandasor Inscription:
- The 5th-century CE Mandasor inscription documents a guild of *silk weavers* (*reshama-vanik*) who migrated from *Lata* (Gujarat) to Dashapura (Mandasor, MP).
- They engaged in diverse pursuits (archery, astronomy, religion) and pooled funds to build a magnificent Sun (*Surya*) temple.
Therefore, Option A is the correct answer.], answer: [A])

#mcq(7, [According to the legal framework of the Manusmriti, which category of marriage forms was considered 'dharmya' (praiseworthy and legally approved)?], (
  [The last four forms (Forms 5 to 8)],
  [The first four forms (Forms 1 to 4)],
  [Only the voluntary romantic union known as Gandharva],
  [All eight forms equally without moral distinction],
))
#solution(7, [Manusmriti Marriage Valuation:
- The Manusmriti outlines eight forms of marriage: the *first four forms* were categorized as *dharmya* (approved/praiseworthy), centered on the gift of a maiden (*kanyadana*).
- Forms 5 to 8 (Asura, Gandharva, Rakshasa, Paishacha) were condemned as adharmya.
Therefore, Option B is the correct answer.], answer: [B])

#mcq(8, [Which tribal forest dweller (Nishada) in the Mahabharata acquired supreme archery skills by practicing before an earthen image of Dronacharya, only to be forced to surrender his right thumb as gurudakshina?], (
